This document is an elaboration on materials and principles taught over the course of several classes at _Klingenspiel_#index[Klingenspiel] in Vienna.
The focus of these lessons were on ways of actively using balance to prepare and initiate actions, especially voids.
To make that possible, however, a fencer must be able to control and manipulate their balance, both in the guard and in transitory and preparatory positions.
For many fencers, however, balance is something they try to retain (to 'keep one's balance'), not something they actively use.
To remedy that, the first section of this document contains drills and exercises aimed at developing an awareness of balance in various positions and movements.
It also contains descriptions of how I manipulate my balance to initiate various common pieces of footwork.
The second section combines these movements into simple solo- and partner drills.
The third and final section contains partner drills that gradually build from simple action/reaction to something resembling constrained sparring.

=== Fundamentals
The human body has two centres of balance.
One, the head, is gimballed, the other, the 'centre of gravity', is located somewhere around the navel; moving either causes the rest of the body to follow.
It can be helpful to think of the rapier as a third centre of balance.
It is not as potent as the other two, but especially a heavier rapier can significantly alter one's balance.
For the purposes of this chapter then, one can think of 'balance'#index[Balance] as the equilibrium between the two centres, the rapier, and whatever is counteracting gravity---usually one or both feet.
The distance and orientation of your feet, your 'base', is another important factor.
Standing on both feet, set roughly shoulder-width apart provides a stable platform, standing on the toes of a single foot does not.
As we shall see, however, stability is not always desirable, and neither is a broad, stable base.

The first step toward intentionally manipulating one's balance is to become sensitive to it.
Therefore, before working on any of the material below---preferably before reading any further---take your rapier and move as you usually would, observing your balance as you do so.
As you work through the rest of this chapter, endeavour to come back to this awareness; it is the basis for everything below.

===== Guard#index[Guard]
While this chapter is agnostic to the style of your guard, but there are a some things that I have found helpful: #footnote[For a thorough discussion of rapier guards, see @finding-the-guard, as well as @developing-the-guard.]
- Place the majority of your weight on the balls of your feet. This does not mean lifting your heels of the floor, however!
- While in your guard, slowly move your one or both of your centres of balance in a direction until you cross the point where you can no longer stay in place and have to catch yourself.
- Practice moving right up to that 'point of no return' and stay there.
- Change a small detail---the angle of your back foot, the distance between your feet ('North/South' and or 'East/West') and observe how that changes any of the above.

When working through the rest of this chapter, periodically come back to the guard and see if anything needs altering.

===== Drop step#index[Drop step]
The drop step is used across martial arts, but I became aware of it through the work of Rory Miller#index[Miller, Rory].#footnote[See also #link("https://chirontraining.blogspot.com/2011/10/drop-step.html").]
In essence, a drop step in an intentional free fall.

===== Altered drop step#index[Drop step]
Shift weight and lean, then drop-step

===== Lunge#index[Lunge]
Arm, lean/shift, drop step, catch. Do not push off back foot.

===== Traverso R#index[Traverso]
As lunge, but different direction

===== Traverso L#index[Traverso]
As trav R, but to the other side. Leave blade in place.

===== Passata#index[Passata]
As lunge + trav L.

===== Passata Sotto#index[Passata Sotto]
Shift weight back, then drop step

===== Back lunge
Shift weight back with a turn, then as lunge.
This is a rare movement for a reason, but a workable alternative for anyone unable to do a passata sotto.

===== Incartata (Volte)#index[Incartata]/Scanzo#index[Scanzo]
Shift weight, then ‘throw’ shoulder.

===== Gathering Step
Different ways of finishing—as a regular step, or any of the movements above.


=== Simple Drills
TK. These drills work regardless of whether both fencers hold their weapon in the same hand or not.

===== On Cue
This drill is a simplified version of classic _salle_ footwork and should be done with weapons.
The instructor is limited to four signs: lunge, traverso left, incartata, and passata sotto.
The goal is to help the students find a guard that lets them move in all directions.

===== On Cue with Gathering Step
As the drill above, but the instructor can also signal a gathering step forward or backward (see 'Gathering Step' above).
For the purposes of this drill, I use the regular signals for 'step forwards' and 'step backwards'.

===== On Cue with Free Steps
As 'On Cue' above, but instead of standing still, the students move freely backwards and forwards.
The goal is to train changes of direction, and retaining control of one's balance while in motion.

===== Stick I
Partner drill.
Both partners place the palm of the weapon hand against opposite ends of a dowel or broomstick.
One leads, the other follows using lunges, traversi left, incartata, and back lunges.
The goal is to not drop the stick.
A certain amount of pressure on the stick helps you feel what your partner is doing.

===== Stick II
Same as above, but adding steps forwards and backwards.

===== Stick III
Same as Stick II, but there is no designated leader and follower.
Think of it as both partners following, not both partners leading.

===== Bind I--III
The same as Stick I--III, but using rapiers in the bind.
Once this works, add disengages.

===== Voiding#index[Voiding]
Partner drill.
A stands in their guard, B attacks with a thrust in opposition on the inside or the outside.
If the attack is on the inside, A voids with a incartata or scanzo; if the attack is on the outside, A voids with a traverso left.
In both cases, A 'leaves' their blade in the middle as described above.

===== Voiding in Motion
As above, but A is freely stepping backwards and forwards.
B keeps the distance until they attack.

=== Complex Drills

Each of the following drills is made up of a series of steps and are designed to be practised progressively—first step one, once that works at full speed, step one and two, then one and two and three, etc.
Start each iteration slower than you think and speed it up to something like sparring speed over 4--8 repetitions.

The goal of each action is to hit the opponent while not getting hit, but it is not the goal of the drill to win at all costs.
Each action should counter the previous action, but especially as speed and intensity increase, not every action will be successful.
That is fine.
Analyse what went wrong and try again.
As with the simple drills, focus on balance and on using it to initiate each action.

These drills encourages a style of fencing that emphasises control over explosiveness.
Control does not mean strength, however.
A weaker fencer with good structure and good technique (strong on weak, etc.) has little issue controlling a much stronger fencer.
Nonetheless, fencers who have based their style on being faster than their opponents may find it frustrating.

==== First Drill
Fencers (A and B) start in middle distance (where either can hit the other with a lunge).
+ A binds onto B’s blade on the inside or outside; B disengages; A rebinds. On a disengage of their
choice, A lunges, striking B with a thrust in opposition instead of rebinding.
+ If A attacks on the inside, B voids with a volte or scanzo; if A attacks on the outside, B voids
with a traverso L. In either case, B ‘leaves’ their blade in the middle line, as described in section
1 above.
+ A parries B’s blade with their empty hand, and strikes B with a stramazone to the head. The
stramazone can be done with or without a step of the back foot.

==== Second Drill
Fencers start at a distance where either can strike the other with a gathering step and a lunge.
+ A binds onto B’s blade on the inside or outside with a gathering step; B disengages and lunges, striking A with a thrust in opposition.
+ If B attacks on the inside, A voids with a volte or scanzo; if B attacks on the outside, A voids with a traverso L. In either case, A ‘leaves’ their blade in the middle line, as described in section 1 above.
+ As 2 above, but instead of disengaging and striking, B may instead take a step backwards. If they do, A finishes the gathering step as a step forward, effectively resetting the situation.
+ As 3 above, but from the initial position, either fencer may initiate the action with a gathering step. At this point, this drill begins to resemble a constrained for of sparring, and the intensity should eventually reflect that.

==== Third Drill
This drill combines elements of the two drills above. Fencers start at a distance where either can strike the other with a gathering step and a lunge.
+ A binds onto B’s blade on the inside or outside with a gathering step; B disengages and lunges, striking A with a thrust in opposition.
+ A either takes B’s disengage as a tempo to strike them with a thrust in opposition,1 or voids B’s attack with a volte/scanzo or traverso L, depending on whether B attacks on the inside or the outside.
+ If A attacks into B’s disengage, B voids A’s attack with a volte/scanzo or traverso L.
+ If B voids, A parries B’s blade with their empty hand, and strikes B with a stramazone to the head. The stramazone can be done with or without a step of the back foot. If A voids, B parries with the empty hand, etc.
+ Instead of disengaging and striking when a seeks the bind, B may take a step backwards. If they do, A finishes the gathering step as a step forward, effectively resetting the situation.
+ As 5 above, but from the initial position, either fencer may initiate the action by seeking the bind with a gathering step. Adding this step makes for an extremely complex drill and in most cases it is best left out.

==== Improvising Drills
Once two fencers have repertoire of actions and are comfortable moving within the idiom of their style#index[Style] (whether that is as broad as ‘seventeenth-century Italian rapier’ or as narrow as ‘Rapier after #cite(<capoferroRidolfoCapoFerro>, form: "author")'#index[Capo Ferro, Ridolfo]), they can begin to improvise drills like the ones above. Doing so is a great awareness drill, and can be tailored to reinforce a fencer’s strength or to address their weaknesses. Drills like these can also given a focus other than balance, e.g. moving off line, using the empty hand, blade- foot coordination, etc. I have found the following guidelines to be helpful:
- Agree on a starting distance and action, e.g. a beat and a thrust, seeking a bind, etc. The drills above are ambidextrous and symmetrical, but that is not a requirement.
- Each iteration of the drill should be done slowly and then sped up over 4–8 repetitions.
- Regardless of the speed, every action should be done with full force aiming at good targets.
- Try to keep each ‘turn’ to one or, at most, two actions each.
- Each action after the first should be what Rory Miller #index[Miller, Rory] calls a golden move @miller2016TrainingSuddenViolence: it protects you, hurts your opponent, puts you in a better position, puts your opponent in a worse position.
- Keep the exchanges relatively short (2–5 ‘turns’ at most), otherwise what started as a fencing drill becomes a memory game.
